WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Test 2. This is the IELTS listening test.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.000
You will hear a number of different recordings and you will have to answer questions on what you hear.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:21.000
There will be time for you to read the instructions and questions and you will have a chance to check your work.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.000
All the recordings will be played once only.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:36.000
The test is in four parts. At the end of the test you will be given 10 minutes to transfer your answers to an answer sheet.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:43.000
Now turn to part 1.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:49.000
Part 1. You will hear two friends talking about a guitar group.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:55.000
First you have some time to look at questions 1 to 6.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:31.000
Now listen carefully and answer questions 1 to 6.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Hi Coleman, how are you?

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Good thanks.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:44.000
I wanted to have a chat with you because our friend Josh told me that you've joined a guitar group and it sounds interesting.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.000
I'd really like to learn myself.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Why don't you come along? I'm sure there's room for another person.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Really? So who runs the classes?

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:02.000
He's called a coordinator. His name's Gary Matheson.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:08.000
Let me note that down. Gary... how do you spell his surname?

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:19.000
It's M-A-T-H-I-E-S-O-N.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.000
Right, thanks.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:29.000
He's retired actually, but he's a really nice guy and he used to play in a lot of bands.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Thanks. So how long have you been going?

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.000
About a month now.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.000
And could you play anything before you started?

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.000
I knew a few chords, but that's all.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.000
I'm sure everyone will be better than me.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:54.000
That's what I thought too. When I first spoke to Gary on the phone, he said it was a class for beginners,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:01.000
but I was still worried that everyone would be better than me. But we were all equally hopeless.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:07.000
Oh, that's reassuring. So where do you meet?

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Well, when I joined the group, they were meeting in Gary's home,

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:20.000
but as the group got bigger, he decided to book a room at the college in town. I prefer going there.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:28.000
I know that place. I used to go to tap dancing classes there when I was at secondary school.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:36.000
I haven't been since though, and I can't remember what road it's in. Is it Lock Street?

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:43.000
It's just beyond there, at the bottom of New Street, near the city roundabout.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.000
Yes, of course.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.000
The guitar club is on the first floor in room T347.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:58.000
Right. And when do you meet? Is it at the weekend?

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:09.000
We meet on Thursdays. It used to be 10.30 and that suited me well, but now we meet at 11.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:15.000
The class that's in there before us asked if they could have the room for another 30 minutes.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:23.000
Oh, I see. Well, I'd love to come, but I don't have a guitar.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.000
Well, you can always buy a second-hand one.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:35.000
There's a website called The Perfect Instrument that sells all kinds of guitars, violins and so on.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.000
I'm sure you'll find something there.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Before you hear the rest of the conversation, you have some time to look at questions 7 to 10.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:05:17.000
So, what's a typical lesson like with Gary?

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:26.000
Well, he always starts by getting us to tune our guitars. That takes about five minutes.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:27.000
Uh-huh.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:33.000
Some people have an app they use, but others do it by ear.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:42.000
Gary goes round and helps them, and while he's doing that, he tells us what he's going to do during the lesson.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.000
Right.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:49.000
First, we usually spend about ten minutes doing some strumming.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:57.000
So, is that using, oh, what are they called, plectrums?

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.000
No, we just use our thumbs.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.000
Oh, much easier.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:09.000
Gary reminds us where to put our fingers for each chord, and then we play them together.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:16.000
Sometimes we all just start laughing because we're so bad at keeping time.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.000
So, Gary starts clapping to help us.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.000
Do you learn to play any songs?

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:28.000
Yes, we do at least one song with words and chords.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:31.000
I mean, that's harder than you think.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.000
Oh, I'm sure it is.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:38.000
That part of the lesson takes about 15 minutes.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:44.000
He often brings a recording of the song and plays it to us first.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:55.000
Then he hands out the song, and if there's a new chord in it, we practise that before we play it together, but really slowly.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:58.000
Do you do any finger-picking?

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:06.000
That's the last ten minutes of the lesson, when we pick out the individual notes from a tune he's made up.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.000
It's always quite simple.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.000
That must be hard though.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:17.000
It is, but people like it because they can really concentrate.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:22.000
And if we're all playing well, it sounds quite impressive.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:30.000
The only trouble is that he sometimes gets us to play one at a time, you know, alone.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.000
Oh, that's scary.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:37.000
It is, but I've got used to it now.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:43.000
At the end, he spends about five minutes telling us what to practise for the following week.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:46.000
Well, thanks, Coleman.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.000
I'll go and have a look at that website, I think.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:55.000
That is the end of part one.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:59.000
You now have one minute to check your answers to part one.

